class:: SpecComplexity
summary:: Count or weighted count of spectral peaks
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/SpecCentroid2, Classes/SpecFlatness2

description::
Counts local maxima above threshold * max(w). A maximum must exceed its left neighbor and equal or exceed its right neighbor; DC and Nyquist are excluded. Weighted mode sums w[peak] / max(w). Threshold is relative, so uniform input gain does not change the count.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Independent local-maximum spectral-complexity implementation, following the peak-count descriptor family discussed by Peeters (2004).

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures count or weighted count of spectral peaks instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: threshold
Relative threshold against the largest weight, clipped to 0..1. Default 0.1.

argument:: weighted
0 counts peaks; positive values sum their relative heights.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecComplexity counts the number of prominent local spectral peaks exceeding a relative amplitude threshold. Dense harmonic chords produce many peaks, whereas sparse solo tones produce few. In this example, spectral complexity opens an expansive stereo reverb tail during dense chordal passages, while sparse passages remain clean and dry. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: displays real-time spectral complexity.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("piano-chords.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic chords fallback
            Mix.ar(SinOsc.ar([261.63, 329.63, 392.00, 523.25] * LFNoise0.kr(0.5).range(1, 1.25))) * 0.15
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var complexity = SpecComplexity.kr(chain, threshold: 0.1, weighted: 0, minFreq: 80, maxFreq: 8000, fftSize: 1024);
        var revMix = (complexity / 20).clip(0, 0.8).lag(0.1);
        var wet = FreeVerb2.ar(source, source, 1.0, 0.8, 0.2) * revMix;
        var sound = (source * (1 - (revMix * 0.4)) ! 2) + wet;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, complexity.clip(0, 50)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) * 0.35
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Spectral Complexity", ~miraBus, 0.0, 30.0, "peaks");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
